module product.fabric.supply

import std.types { Timestamp, Int }
import std.measure { MoneyAmountMicro, MoneyPerSecond, MoneyPerHour, MoneyOnce }
import std.currency { CurrencyCode }
import product.compute_fabric { Shape }
import product.fabric.identity { FabricIdentity, OfferKey }
import product.fabric.demand { ObservationReceiptRef }
import product.fabric.work { CapabilityManifestRef, TrustDomainRef }

// An Offer is a supplier's statement of capacity it is willing to sell. Owned
// capacity and rented capacity are ONE type: what differs is the evidence behind
// the statement, not the kind of thing being stated.

// Observed and quoted supply differ in their evidence, not in their type, and
// minting two Offer types would fork the selection fold that must compare
// them. ObservedSupply is a measurement - this host has three free slots right
// now, and here is the receipt. QuotedSupply is a standing willingness to sell
// - this supplier will provide up to N of this shape at this rate until this
// time, and here is the quote. Bottomless cloud supply is a QuotedSupply with
// a large quantity bound, never an unbounded one: an offer without a bound
// cannot be conserved against, and infinite supply is a claim no supplier
// actually makes. The fields the decision consumes - quantity bound,
// readiness, validity, normalized quote - stay SURFACED on the offer rather
// than hidden inside the evidence; the evidence proves those values, it does
// not replace them.
// An owned offer carries an EXPLICIT ZERO quote and creates a zero-valued
// money reservation, rather than carrying no quote at all. Absence must never
// be interpreted as free. The predecessor model made cost_quote optional and
// every owned host used the absent arm, so a consumer reading it could not
// distinguish costs nothing from nobody priced this - and the safe reading of
// the second is not zero. Requiring the amount means a supplier whose price is
// genuinely unknown has to say so in a way the selection fold can refuse,
// instead of being silently treated as the cheapest offer available.
// A quote is std.measure's MoneyRate at the period the supplier bills in,
// never a second money-per-period record. MoneyRate already carries the amount
// and the currency and already makes the period a type argument, so a rate at
// one period is not assignable where another is expected -- which is exactly
// the property a price needs, and the reason an earlier version of this file
// minting its own carrier was a fork rather than a specialization. That
// version's stated defence was that folding the unit into the measure would
// make two rates addable; MoneyRate does not fold it into the measure, so the
// defence described a carrier that does not exist.
//
// The runtime coproduct is here for a reason the type parameter cannot serve:
// selection ranks offers billed on different bases against each other, so they
// must inhabit one roster, and Offer<P, PerHour> and Offer<P, PerSecond> would
// be different types. Discriminating at the value level keeps the roster
// homogeneous while keeping the periods unaddable, since reaching either
// amount requires naming which period it is.
//
// Normalizing a supplier's published terms into one of these is the supplier
// binding's work, and so are billing quantum, rounding, minimum charge, fixed
// and setup charges, caps, and when billing starts and stops. The core
// compares normalized quotes; it does not model any supplier's tariff.

type OfferQuote =
  | QuotedPerSecond(MoneyPerSecond)
  | QuotedPerHour(MoneyPerHour)
  | QuotedFlatPerGrant(MoneyOnce)

fn offer_quote_amount(q: OfferQuote) -> MoneyAmountMicro {
  match q {
    QuotedPerSecond(r) => r.amount
    QuotedPerHour(r) => r.amount
    QuotedFlatPerGrant(r) => r.amount
  }
}

fn offer_quote_currency(q: OfferQuote) -> CurrencyCode {
  match q {
    QuotedPerSecond(r) => r.currency
    QuotedPerHour(r) => r.currency
    QuotedFlatPerGrant(r) => r.currency
  }
}

type OfferEvidence
  = ObservedSupply { observed_at: Timestamp, receipt: ObservationReceiptRef }
  | QuotedSupply {
      quoted_at: Timestamp,
      valid_until: Timestamp,
      receipt: ObservationReceiptRef,
    }

type Offer<P> {
  id: FabricIdentity<P, OfferKey>
  executor: P
  shape: Shape
  capabilities: CapabilityManifestRef
  trust_domain: TrustDomainRef
  quantity_bound: Int
  ready_at: Timestamp
  quote: OfferQuote
  evidence: OfferEvidence
}

// An Offer carries no remaining_capacity field. Remaining capacity is DERIVED
// as the allocatable bound minus the sum of active grant claims against that
// offer, and deriving it is precisely what makes atomic grant issuance
// conserve supply. A stored remainder is a second representation of the grant
// set: it can disagree with the grants that actually exist, and the
// disagreement is invisible until two grants have already been issued against
// capacity that existed only in a stale field.
// Selection is a named policy with one implementation. The interface is named so
// a second policy is a second instantiation rather than an edit to ExecutionGrant or to
// the clearing fold; if adding one later requires touching a core carrier, the
// axis was never real.

// The policy is an axis with exactly one instantiation today: cheapest
// fungible with delay valuation. The ORDER of its filters is load-bearing and
// is not a performance detail. Fungibility is decided FIRST - which offers can
// satisfy this work's shape, capabilities and trust domain, with price not
// consulted - so that price can never rescue an offer that cannot run the
// work. Affordability is decided SECOND: within the demand's reservation price
// and maximum buy order, with budget remaining. Cheapest is decided LAST, over
// the survivors, comparing provider charge plus valued availability delay,
// with a stable deterministic tie-break. This is a posted-offer market and not
// an auction: suppliers publish asks, each demand carries a buyer reservation
// price, and the fabric takes the cheapest admissible ask. No clearing-price
// machinery exists because none is needed to make that decision.
// Rented supply being selected only after owned supply is exhausted is a
// CONSEQUENCE of cheapest-admissible-ask, never a fallback arm, and the
// distinction decides whether cost can run away. A fallback is a failure arm
// that widens when the preferred path is unavailable, and it hides the deficit
// that triggered it. Here nothing widens: owned capacity at an explicit zero
// quote is simply the cheapest admissible ask while any remains, and when none
// remains the same fold evaluates the same predicate over the remaining asks.
// If no ask is admissible - budget exhausted, every ask above the reservation
// price or the maximum buy order, no offer fungible - the outcome is a typed
// refusal or a demand that stays pending. It is never a degraded execution
// path, and there is no arm to reach for one.

type SelectionPolicy =
  | CheapestFungibleWithDelayValuation
